    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "17.2: Info Gap: What's the Transformation?",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Suppose we considered the function \\(T\\) which is the sum of \\(Q\\) and \\(S\\). Is \\(T\\) also periodic? If yes, what is its period? If no, explain why not.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here is the graph of \\(f(x)=\\cos(x)\\) and the graph of \\(g\\), which is a transformation of \\(f\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Identify a transformation that takes \\(f\\) to \\(g\\) and write an equation for \\(g\\) in terms of \\(f\\) matching the transformation.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Identify at least one other transformation that takes \\(f\\) to \\(g\\) and write an equation for \\(g\\) in terms of \\(f\\) matching the transformation.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here are graphs of two trigonometric functions:",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The function \\(f\\) is given by \\(f(x) = \\sin(x)\\). How can we transform the graph of \\(f\\) to look like the graph of \\(g\\)? Looking at the graph of \\(f\\), we need to make the period and the amplitude smaller, translate the graph up, and…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The amplitude of \\(g\\) is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) and the period is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) so we can begin by changing \\(\\sin(x)\\) to \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\sin(4x)\\). The midline of \\(g\\) is 2.5 so we need a vertical translation of 2.5, giving us…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Another way to think about the transformation is to first notice that \\(g\\) has a minimum when \\(x\\) is 0. If we translate \\(\\sin(x)\\) right by \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), then \\(\\sin(x-\\frac{\\pi}{2})\\) also has a minimum at \\(x=0\\). The period of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The maximum distance of the values of a periodic function above or below the midline.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L17-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "17.3: Match the Graph",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The value halfway between the maximum and minimum values of a period function. Also the horizontal line whose \\(y\\)-coordinate is that value.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l17.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
